The Electricity Has Opened a Bakery

A satirical inquiry into selling people the means of discovery, then beating them to the discovery

Journal of Applied Own Goals — Special Issue: Customer Empowerment

All boardroom dialogue below is invented. The argument assumes, for the sake of examining the business decision, that OpenAI produced its mathematical work independently.

Abstract

This paper examines an unusual commercial proposition: persuading researchers to buy artificial intelligence so they can achieve extraordinary things, then deploying a more powerful internal system to achieve those things before they do.

We consider the strongest possible defence of the arrangement: no research was stolen, no private manuscript was borrowed, and every relevant line was independently produced. Under these favourable assumptions, a substantial question remains:

Why has the company selling the ladder entered the climbing competition in a helicopter?

The paper identifies several potential sources of confusion, including the existence of frontiers beyond the advertised frontier, the reclassification of customers as benchmark opponents, and the possibility that empowering humanity has been interpreted as empowering the portion of humanity with an employee pass.

We conclude that winning a research race and strengthening a research business are distinct achievements. Confusing them may produce a spectacular announcement followed by an awkward conversation with Accounts.

1. Introduction: the entirely reasonable expectation that electricity will remain electricity

In March 2026, Sam Altman described a future in which intelligence would be sold as a utility, comparing it to electricity and water, purchased according to usage. It is an attractive proposition: powerful infrastructure, broadly available, enabling people to build things of their own. 

Consider a baker.

The baker pays the electricity company. The electricity company powers the ovens. The baker develops recipes, trains staff, takes commercial risks and sells bread.

One morning, the electricity company arrives wearing an apron.

“Good news,” it announces. “We have demonstrated the extraordinary potential of electricity.”

“How?”

“We have opened a bakery.”

“Opposite mine?”

“Yes.”

“With the same electricity you sell me?”

“An experimental form of electricity. Considerably more electrical.”

“Can I buy it?”

“Not presently.”

“And this demonstrates?”

“How much more bread can be made with electricity.”

“I already knew that. That is why I was paying you.”

The company issues a statement expressing disappointment that the baking community has become resistant to progress.

A spokesperson explains that the ovens have never been more aligned.

2. Methodology: let us assume nobody stole a bloody thing

OpenAI announced its claimed Navier–Stokes solution on 8 September 2026. Its account says the effort began on 1 September after rumours of other researchers’ progress. In its 10 September update, the company said Tristan Buckmaster’s recent Codex prompts could not have influenced its system, including through training. 

That chronology understandably invites scrutiny. A supplier learning that researchers are approaching something important, then mounting its own effort, is an arrangement capable of looking suspicious even before anyone establishes what information passed where.

But this paper grants independent discovery.

Let the manuscripts be untouched. Let the private conversations remain private. Let the provenance investigation return a clean result.

The commercial question survives intact.

A customer can be unhappy that their supplier has become their competitor without alleging that the supplier burgled the laboratory.

This distinction appears to require its own department.

At the Department of Entirely Separate Questions, an official receives the following complaint:

“I bought this service to help me complete my research. Its provider then entered the same race using resources I cannot access.”

The official consults a form.

“Was anything stolen?”

“That is not the question I am asking.”

The official consults the form more intensely.

“Was anything stolen?”

“I am asking whether this is a sensible relationship between a research platform and the researchers it wants to attract.”

The official stamps the document:

NO THEFT ALLEGED. CUSTOMER SATISFIED.

The customer is escorted out for continuing to exist after the complaint has been resolved.

3. The Ministry of Several Frontiers

ChatGPT Pro’s December 2024 launch marketing promised scaled access to “the best of OpenAI’s models and tools”, beneath the description “Broadening usage of frontier AI”. Those are the published marketing words being examined here. 

The word frontier now requires a small administrative complex.

A customer approaches the desk.

“I would like access to the frontier.”

“Certainly. You are standing at it.”

“What is that over there?”

“Another frontier.”

“Beyond this one?”

“Yes.”

“Then surely that is the frontier.”

“Not for billing purposes.”

Behind the counter hangs a map. It identifies the Public Frontier, the Pro Frontier, the Internal Frontier and an area marked Here Be Models We Will Tell You About After They Have Won.

The customer points to the farthest boundary.

“Is that the best one?”

“It is the best one we have.”

“I thought I was buying access to the best one you have.”

“You are buying access to the best one we have made available for you to buy.”

“Could you put that sentence on the advertisement?”

The official presses a concealed button.

A representative from the Department of Less Exciting Wording arrives and confiscates the map.

The commercial question becomes especially sharp when a private prototype is used as the supplier’s entrant in a race against researchers purchasing its released tools. At that point, the distinction between available capability and actual capability becomes relevant to what customers thought they were building their work around.

There is a difference between discovering that the manufacturer has next year’s engine in development and discovering that it has entered next year’s engine against you in this year’s race.

Particularly when it sold you your car under the slogan:

Go further than ever before.

Technically, it did not specify further than whom.

4. Experimental design: the laptop versus the building

OpenAI describes the system behind its claimed result as using a model significantly more capable than GPT‑6 Astra. It reports roughly 10,000 concurrent agents in the successful group and approximately 130 billion output tokens during the Navier–Stokes effort. 

These are consequential experimental conditions.

They are not a decorative footnote beneath a photograph of someone looking unusually intelligent.

A comparison that changes the model, computational resources, parallelism and coordination does not isolate the contribution of any single factor. It establishes what the whole arrangement produced under those conditions.

Nevertheless, our imaginary press conference proceeds.

“Ladies and gentlemen, we have compared a researcher using a retail subscription with an internal system supported by an enormous computational operation.”

“And what have you discovered?”

“The internal system finished first.”

“Did you hold the resources constant?”

“No.”

“The model?”

“No.”

“The number of simultaneous attempts?”

“No.”

“What, precisely, was controlled?”

“The announcement.”

The company has demonstrated an impressive machine. The public is then invited to admire a rather broader proposition: that the people outside the building have been intellectually overtaken by the people who own the building.

Accounts requests clarification.

“Have we established that the customer could not do it?”

“We established that we did it first.”

“With the same equipment?”

“No.”

“So we have established that we can run the better-equipped operation?”

“Please stop making the achievement sound like something involving equipment.”

“But there appears to be rather a lot of equipment.”

Accounts is reminded that this is a discussion about intelligence.

The equipment is moved out of shot.

5. Imagined board minutes: “Sam, are we sure this is a good idea?”

Nature’s reporting describes Buckmaster’s work with Levent Alpöge, who is affiliated with Anthropic, as a personal collaboration. That distinction matters: a mathematician’s employer and the ownership or purpose of a particular research project are not automatically the same thing. 

Our entirely fictional board meeting begins with that distinction already experiencing difficulties.

“Some mathematicians may be making progress.”

“Excellent. Are they using AI?”

“Yes.”

“Then our vision is working.”

“One of them works at Anthropic.”

A long silence follows.

Someone closes the blinds.

“Then our vision is in danger.”

A junior employee raises a hand.

“Sam, are we sure this is a good idea?”

“Which part?”

“The part where we sell researchers tools to advance their work, then mobilise a much stronger system to get there before them.”

“We are demonstrating capability.”

“To whom?”

“Everyone.”

“Including the researchers?”

“Especially the researchers.”

“What are we demonstrating to them?”

“That they need our technology.”

“They already bought our technology.”

“Yes, but now they know how much better our technology can be.”

“Can they have that technology?”

“Not yet.”

“So the demonstration is that buying our technology does not give them the technology we will use to compete with them?”

The employee is thanked for identifying a communications challenge.

Another person suggests inviting five or ten external experts.

“Before the project?”

“Yes.”

“With genuine influence over the research?”

“Yes.”

“And access to the resources?”

“Yes.”

“And an agreed publication process?”

“Yes.”

“And the possibility that it becomes their achievement, supported by us?”

The room becomes very quiet.

“That sounds dangerously like facilitating science.”

“Wasn’t that the proposal?”

“The proposal was to empower people.”

“These are people.”

“Yes, but some of them have other email addresses.”

A compromise is suggested: invite the experts later, when they can provide the valuable external perspective of standing beside the announcement.

The junior employee tries once more.

“Could the researchers lead the programme, with us supplying the models and compute?”

“Possibly. But what would that prove about Anthropic?”

The minute-taker records that the discussion concerned the long-term interests of humanity.

6. Humanity: now conveniently located inside the building

The phrase empowering humanity is admirably expansive.

Unfortunately, it becomes easier to administer when humanity is reduced to a list maintained by Human Resources.

In our fictional organisation, the original mission statement reads:

Give people the means to achieve things they could not otherwise achieve.

Following a strategic review, it becomes:

Achieve things people could not otherwise achieve.

The words give people the means are removed to improve efficiency.

Nobody notices immediately because the revised version still contains the word people.

Soon, a delegation arrives to ask whether the distinction matters.

There is a real-world counterpart to that delegation: on 11 September, 25 Fields medallists issued a declaration arguing that the objectives of AI companies and the mathematical community were severely misaligned. They raised concerns about corporate benchmarking, attribution, understanding and the development of mathematical ideas. 

In the imaginary reception area, the declaration is handed to Customer Success.

“Twenty-five Fields medallists,” says the receptionist.

“Have they tried a more specific prompt?”

“They are questioning our objectives.”

“Ask them to provide an example of the desired output.”

“They want a different relationship between the technology and the people doing mathematics.”

“Have they refreshed the page?”

There is no obligation to endorse every sentence of the declaration to recognise the problem illustrated by this exchange.

The customers may want extremely powerful AI.

They may want faster discoveries.

They may want ambitious scientific work.

They may simply have expected to be participants in that future, rather than the audience at the product demonstration explaining why their own participation is becoming optional.

That is a difficult distinction to communicate to a department whose principal measure of empowerment is how much the company can now do without you.

7. The finite-time blow-up of the customer base

At this point, someone should invite Accounts back into the room.

Not to evaluate a mathematical proof.

To evaluate a business model.

For the purposes of a deliberately simple model, let subscription or API revenue be

R=N\bar{s},

where N is the number of paying customers and \bar{s} is their average spending over a specified period.

The equation contains two quantities.

The board has prepared an extensive strategy for increasing the second.

Accounts would like to discuss the first.

“What happens,” asks Mr Pargeter, “if the customers conclude that building valuable work around our platform exposes them to competition from a supplier they cannot match?”

“They will need more AI.”

“Or they may choose another supplier.”

“Then we will demonstrate that our AI is better.”

“That addresses capability. I am asking about the relationship.”

“We can make the demonstration larger.”

Mr Pargeter removes his glasses.

“Could we demonstrate that it is commercially sensible to trust us?”

There is a pause.

“Does that run on the new model?”

The difficulty is that researchers, engineers and businesses are not merely an audience for announcements. In the customer-enabling business model, they are meant to turn assistance into work that matters to them—and, in some cases, into income with which to purchase more assistance.

A press release does not manufacture a pump, commission a factory or persuade a customer to buy a product.

Where mathematical research has an industrial application, identifying that application and making it work requires further effort. The people performing that effort need resources, reasons to participate and some prospect of retaining value from what they build.

Otherwise, the proposed economic cycle becomes unusual:

The platform enables the customer.

The platform overtakes the customer.

The customer loses confidence in building around the platform.

The platform asks why the customer has not increased their spending.

In the imaginary boardroom, an engineer enters carrying a valve.

“What is that?” asks Marketing.

“A physical object.”

“Why?”

“Someone has to make the application work.”

“Can the model do it?”

“It can help. We still need to test this.”

“Can the testing be replaced by a statement about the pace of progress?”

“No.”

“Has anyone asked the stronger model?”

The engineer explains that a leaking valve does not become watertight when informed that its manufacturer has won a benchmark.

This is entered in the minutes as an unexpected limitation of the physical world.

The company can, of course, choose a different strategy: become a downstream competitor and capture more of the work itself.

But that is a different proposition from being the infrastructure on which independent people build their own successes.

“We will help you build your business” and “we may build your business better than you can” are different sales pitches.

The second might be profitable.

It is unlikely to improve the first by remaining unmentioned.

8. Discussion: the revolutionary possibility of helping the customer finish

There was another possible demonstration.

Select a researcher-led programme. Supply exceptional computing resources. Establish responsibility, attribution and publication arrangements before the race starts. Let external expertise influence what is attempted and how the result is assessed.

Then demonstrate what those people accomplished with the technology.

This would test a proposition directly relevant to the customer:

What can someone achieve when this company genuinely backs their work?

It might produce an extraordinary result quickly. It might take longer. A researcher-led outcome two years later could still be preferable to an earlier announcement that damages the willingness of researchers to work with the provider.

The two years are not the objective. The quality of the relationship is.

Elapsed time to a headline is one measure. The number of people enabled to pursue consequential work is another. The amount of trust retained is another still.

In our imaginary organisation, this alternative is rejected on the grounds that it would allow the customers to look impressively capable.

“That,” says Mr Pargeter, “was what I thought we were selling.”

He is informed that the meeting has overrun.

Conclusion: please stop competing with the people buying the electricity

The strongest criticism here does not require a stolen manuscript.

Grant independent discovery. Grant a technically remarkable result. Grant exceptional engineering.

The business question remains:

Why should ambitious people build their futures around a supplier that may enter their field with stronger tools than it will let them use?

A company can answer that question through its conduct, its access arrangements and the boundaries it establishes between supporting customers and competing with them.

It cannot answer it merely by winning another race.

Back at the bakery, the electricity company unveils its final announcement.

“We have demonstrated that we can produce more bread than every customer on this street.”

“Impressive,” says the baker.

“We have transformed the industry.”

“So I see.”

“We expect substantial growth in electricity demand.”

“From whom?”

The company looks down the street.

Every bakery is closed.

For the first time that morning, someone calls Accounts.

The electricity is excellent. The business model appears to be on fire.
